\DOC REABBREV_TAC

\TYPE {REABBREV_TAC : term -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Given an equation {v = t} whose right-hand side {t} already occurs as the
left-hand side of an assumption {t = u}, replaces that assumption by {t = v}
together with {u = v}, and rewrites {u} to {v} in the conclusion.

\DESCRIBE
The argument {tm : term} is an equation of the form {v = t}, where {v} is the
new name to abbreviate with and {t} is a term occurring as the left-hand side
of an existing assumption. The assumption {t = u} is dropped from {A}, and the
two assumptions {t = v} and the defining equation {u = v} are appended at the
end; all other assumptions keep their order and content. In the conclusion
every occurrence of {u} is replaced by {v}; where {u} does not occur in the
conclusion, the conclusion is unchanged.
{
   A ?- t'
   ========================  REABBREV_TAC `v = t`
   A' ?- t'[v/u]
}
\noindent where {A} contains {t = u} and {A'} is {A} with {t = u} dropped and
{t = v}, {u = v} appended.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces only this kind of change occurs, always on goals whose
assumptions contain a matching equation.

\ENDDOC
